🧾 FEATURE CONTRACT 08 — COMPANY & USER-COMPANY RELATIONSHIPS

Version: 3.2.1
Status: IMPLEMENTATION-READY
Type: Feature Contract / Architecture & Implementation Specification
Date: 2026-02-12
Author: Senior Product Architect + CTO
Scope: Rails API-only / Domain / Data / REST API / TDD / RSwag
Quality Target: PLATINUM

1. Purpose

FC-08 establishes the Company domain and the explicit relationship between a User and a Company.

The feature provides the legal and organizational foundation required for Foresy to distinguish:

the person using Foresy (User);
the legal entity through which an activity is conducted (Company);
the role in which a User acts with respect to a Company (UserCompany);
the business activities subsequently associated with that relationship.

FC-08 is intentionally limited to this foundation.

It does not implement the future forecasting, simulation, fiscal calculation, notification, accounting, or CRA evolution mechanisms.

2. Product Context

Foresy is a backend-first SaaS platform for independent professionals.

The product must eventually allow a User to:

operate as an Independent through a Company;
act as a Client through another Company;
potentially act in several roles for the same Company;
operate with several Companies;
use Foresy before having an active Company;
distinguish simulation from actual business activity;
build a legally and fiscally meaningful history of activity.

FC-08 establishes the structural foundations for these capabilities.

3. Relationship With Previous Feature Contracts
3.1 FC-05 — Rate Limiting

FC-08 must preserve all security and rate-limiting mechanisms established by FC-05.

No bypass or modification is introduced.

3.2 FC-06 — Missions

FC-06 established the Mission domain.

A Mission:

belongs to a Client Company;
involves an Independent;
represents a business relationship/activity.

FC-08 provides the Company and UserCompany structures upon which future Mission relationships can rely.

FC-08 MUST NOT refactor FC-06.

3.3 FC-07 — CRA

FC-07 established the CRA domain as the source of truth for realized activity.

FC-08 does not modify CRA behavior.

The following concepts remain outside FC-08:

forecast engine;
scenario engine;
forecast → realized conversion;
automatic daily CRA transformation;
CRA lifecycle evolution;
fiscal calculations based on CRA.

These concepts will be addressed by future Feature Contracts.

4. Architectural Principles

FC-08 strictly follows the global Foresy architecture.

4.1 Relation-Driven Design

Aucune entité métier ne porte de clé étrangère vers une autre entité métier.

Relations between business domains are represented by explicit relation entities.

Therefore:

User
 │
 │
 ▼
UserCompany
 │
 │
 ▼
Company

But:

User
 ──X──> Company

is forbidden.

Company MUST NOT contain:

user_id

User MUST NOT contain:

company_id
5. Domain Boundaries

FC-08 introduces or formalizes two business concepts.

5.1 Company

Represents a legal or organizational entity used within Foresy.

Company is an autonomous domain entity.

It contains no direct reference to User.

5.2 UserCompany

Represents the relationship between a User and a Company.

The relationship carries contextual information, particularly:

role

This allows the same User to have different roles depending on the Company and the relationship.

6. Company Concept

A Company represents an entity through which business activity can be contextualized.

Examples include:

the User's own company;
a client company;
another company with which the User has a business relationship.

The Company itself does not determine the User's role.

That information belongs to UserCompany.

7. User Concept

User represents the authenticated person using Foresy.

A User may:

have no Company relationship;
have one Company relationship;
have multiple Companies;
have several roles;
have several roles with the same Company.

The architecture must not impose an artificial global role on the User.

8. UserCompany Concept

UserCompany is an explicit relation entity.

It answers:

"How does this User act in relation to this Company?"

The minimum contextual information is:

user_id
company_id
role
deleted_at
9. Roles

FC-08 defines the following roles.

9.1 independent

The User acts as an Independent through the Company.

This relationship represents the User's own professional activity.

9.2 client

The User acts as a Client / project owner through the Company.

This allows a User to engage an Independent for a Mission.

10. Multi-Role Principle

A User may simultaneously hold multiple roles with the same Company.

Example:

User A
 │
 ├── independent → Company X
 │
 └── client      → Company X

This is explicitly permitted.

The architecture MUST NOT enforce:

UNIQUE(user_id, company_id)

because this would prevent multiple roles.

Instead, uniqueness applies to:

UNIQUE(user_id, company_id, role)

Therefore:

User A + Company X + independent
User A + Company X + client

are both valid.

But:

User A + Company X + independent
User A + Company X + independent

is forbidden.

11. Multiple Companies

A User may be related to multiple Companies.

Example:

User A
 │
 ├── independent → Company 1
 ├── client      → Company 1
 ├── independent → Company 2
 └── client      → Company 3

No global Company limitation is introduced by FC-08.

Business-specific constraints concerning the number or nature of Companies are outside this Feature Contract.

12. Temporal Role Evolution

Roles may change over time.

FC-08 does not introduce a complete temporal model for UserCompany.

The current MVP relationship is represented by the active/inactive state through:

deleted_at

Historical temporal modeling beyond soft deletion is explicitly deferred.

This avoids premature modeling while preserving the possibility of future evolution.

13. User Without Company

A User may exist without any Company.

This is a valid and supported state.

For example:

User
 │
 └── no UserCompany

This is particularly important during onboarding.

The User must not be forced to create a Company merely to complete registration.

14. Simulation Without Company

Foresy must support the conceptual state where a User does not yet have an active Company.

However:

A simulation is not a Company.

FC-08 MUST NOT create a fictitious Company merely to represent a simulation.

Therefore:

User
 │
 └── simulation

does not imply:

Company

Future simulation features will model this independently.

15. Company Creation

Company creation is performed atomically with creation of the corresponding UserCompany relationship when initiated through the onboarding flow.

The transaction must guarantee:

Company + UserCompany

or:

nothing

A Company MUST NOT be left orphaned by a failed relationship creation.

16. Company Lifecycle

Company uses the existing Foresy soft-delete strategy.

The lifecycle is represented by:

deleted_at

No new status or archived_at field is introduced.

The same principle applies to UserCompany.

17. Active Records

Active records are those where:

deleted_at IS NULL

Inactive records are those where:

deleted_at IS NOT NULL

No default_scope may be introduced.

Explicit scopes are required.

For example:

Company.active
Company.deleted
UserCompany.active
UserCompany.deleted
