23. UserCompany Foreign Keys

The Relation-Driven principle does not prohibit the relation table itself from referencing the entities it relates.

Therefore:

user_companies.user_id → users.id
user_companies.company_id → companies.id

are valid.

The prohibition applies to direct foreign keys from one business entity to another.

Thus:

companies.user_id
users.company_id

remain forbidden.

24. UserCompany Role Storage

The existing PostgreSQL enum is preserved:

user_company_role_enum

The logical application type is a role string.

The PostgreSQL implementation is an enum.

No rollback to a generic string column is permitted.

Current valid values:

independent
client

Future roles may be introduced through a future Feature Contract.

25. UserCompany Uniqueness

The database MUST enforce:

UNIQUE(user_id, company_id, role)

This guarantees:

multiple Companies per User;
multiple roles per Company;
no duplicate identical relationship.
26. Soft Delete on UserCompany

UserCompany receives:

deleted_at

Soft deletion does not physically remove the historical relationship.

An active relationship is:

deleted_at IS NULL

A deleted relationship is:

deleted_at IS NOT NULL

The implementation MUST use explicit scopes.

No default_scope.

27. Re-activation

FC-08 does not define automatic reactivation behavior.

If a deleted relationship needs to be recreated, this behavior must be explicitly implemented and tested rather than inferred.

No implicit resurrection is allowed.

28. Data Provenance

FC-08 MVP treats Company information as declarative application data.

No field-level provenance model is introduced.

The following are outside scope:

source
retrieved_at
source_per_field
INSEE synchronization
INPI synchronization
historical official snapshots

These can be introduced in a future dedicated Feature Contract.

29. INSEE / INPI Integration

FC-08 prepares the domain for future integration with:

INSEE;
INPI;
official legal-entity data.

However, FC-08 does not perform external synchronization.

No external API call is required to create a Company.

No external dependency must be introduced into the Company creation transaction.

30. Company Does Not Represent Simulation

A Company always represents a real organizational/legal context within the Company domain.

FC-08 does not create:

demo_company
fake_company
simulation_company

to support onboarding.

Simulation is a separate domain concern.

31. No Company Requirement During Signup

A User may complete signup without creating a Company.

Therefore:

POST /users

does not require:

company_id

and Company creation is a subsequent operation.

32. Company Authorization

A User may access or modify a Company only through an active UserCompany relationship.

Authorization MUST be relation-based.

The API MUST NOT infer authorization from:

companies.user_id

because that field does not exist.

33. UserCompany Authorization

A User may manage a UserCompany relationship only when authorized by the authenticated User context.

Cross-user manipulation MUST be rejected.

Examples:

User A cannot modify User B's UserCompany.
34. API Philosophy

The API remains RESTful.

Primary resources:

/companies
/user_companies

The API must expose the domain relationships explicitly.

35. Company Endpoints
35.1 List Companies
GET /companies

Returns Companies accessible to the authenticated User through active UserCompany relationships.

35.2 Create Company
POST /companies

Primary onboarding endpoint.

The operation accepts Company information and the desired initial role.

Example:

{
  "company": {
    "name": "Example Company",
    "siren": "123456789",
    "siret": "12345678900001",
    "legal_form": "5499",
    "vat_regime": "franchise"
  },
  "role": "independent"
}

The operation atomically creates:

Company
+
UserCompany

for the authenticated User.

35.3 Show Company
GET /companies/:id

Returns the Company when the authenticated User has an active relationship with it.

35.4 Update Company
PATCH /companies/:id

Updates permitted Company attributes.

Authorization is relation-based.

35.5 Delete Company
DELETE /companies/:id

Performs a soft delete.

The Company is not physically removed.

36. UserCompany Endpoints
36.1 List Relationships
GET /user_companies

Returns the authenticated User's active relationships.

36.2 Create Relationship
POST /user_companies

Used primarily to add another role to an existing Company.

Example:

{
  "company_id": 42,
  "role": "client"
}
36.3 Update Relationship
PATCH /user_companies/:id

Permitted relationship attributes may be modified according to the contract.

36.4 Delete Relationship
DELETE /user_companies/:id

Performs a soft delete.

37. Atomic Company Creation

POST /companies MUST execute Company and UserCompany creation in one database transaction.

Conceptually:

transaction do
  company = Company.create!(...)
  UserCompany.create!(
    user: current_user,
    company: company,
    role: params[:role]
  )
end

If either operation fails:

Company MUST NOT persist.
UserCompany MUST NOT persist.
38. Parameter Wrapping

The CompaniesController MUST explicitly support Rails parameter wrapping.

Conceptually:

wrap_parameters :company, include: [
  :name,
  :siren,
  :siret,
  :legal_form,
  :tax_number,
  :address_line_1,
  :address_line_2,
  :postal_code,
  :city,
  :country,
  :currency,
  :vat_regime
]
Important

role MUST NOT be included in the Company wrapper.

role belongs to UserCompany, not Company.

Therefore:

params[:role]

and:

params[:company]

are intentionally separate.

The API must support the expected flat/wrapped Rails parameter behavior consistently with the existing application conventions.

39. Validation Strategy

Business validations must exist at both:

application/model level;
database level where applicable.

Application validations provide predictable API errors.

Database constraints provide final integrity guarantees.

Neither layer replaces the other.

40. SIREN Validation

SIREN validation must verify:

presence;
format;
validity;
uniqueness.

The validation must be deterministic and independently tested.

The database must additionally enforce uniqueness.

41. SIRET Validation

SIRET is optional.

When provided:

format must be valid;
validity must be verified;
uniqueness must be enforced.

NULL is valid.

Multiple Companies may therefore have:

siret = NULL
42. Error Handling

The API must return deterministic HTTP responses.

Invalid input must not result in:

partial persistence;
unhandled exceptions;
ambiguous behavior.

Validation failures must return appropriate client errors.

Unauthorized access must not expose another User's Companies or relationships.

43. Security

FC-08 must preserve existing authentication and authorization mechanisms.

The implementation must explicitly test:

unauthenticated access;
authenticated access;
ownership/relationship boundaries;
cross-user access;
manipulation of another User's UserCompany.

No authorization shortcut may be introduced.

44. Gherkin Acceptance Criteria
Scenario 1 — User without Company
Given an authenticated User with no Company
When the User requests their Companies
Then an empty collection is returned
And no Company is automatically created
Scenario 2 — Create Company as Independent
Given an authenticated User
When the User creates a Company with role "independent"
Then the Company is created
And a UserCompany is created
And the UserCompany role is "independent"
And both records are persisted atomically
Scenario 3 — Create Company as Client
Given an authenticated User
When the User creates a Company with role "client"
Then the Company is created
And a UserCompany is created
And the UserCompany role is "client"
Scenario 4 — Multiple Companies
Given an authenticated User
And the User already has a Company
When the User creates another Company
Then both Companies are accessible to the User
And neither Company references the User directly
Scenario 5 — Multiple Roles
Given an authenticated User
And an existing Company
When the User creates a "client" relationship
And an "independent" relationship already exists
Then both relationships coexist
Scenario 6 — Duplicate Role
Given an authenticated User
And an existing "independent" relationship with a Company
When the User attempts to create another "independent" relationship
Then the request is rejected
And no duplicate relationship is created
Scenario 7 — No Fictitious Company
Given an authenticated User without a Company
When the User uses functionality that does not require a real Company
Then no fictitious Company is created
Scenario 8 — Invalid SIREN
Given an authenticated User
When the User attempts to create a Company with an invalid SIREN
Then the request is rejected
And no Company is persisted
And no UserCompany is persisted
Scenario 9 — SIRET Absent
Given an authenticated User
When the User creates a Company without a SIRET
Then the Company is created
And the SIRET is NULL

SIRET is optional for FC-08 MVP.

Scenario 10 — Duplicate SIREN
Given an existing Company with a SIREN
When the User attempts to create another Company with the same SIREN
Then the request is rejected
And no duplicate Company is created
Scenario 11 — Duplicate SIRET
Given an existing Company with a SIRET
When the User attempts to create another Company with the same SIRET
Then the request is rejected
And no duplicate Company is created
Scenario 12 — Atomicity
Given an authenticated User
When Company creation succeeds
And UserCompany creation fails
Then the Company is rolled back
And no orphan Company remains
Scenario 13 — Unauthorized Access
Given User A owns a Company
And User B is authenticated
When User B attempts to access User A's Company
Then access is rejected
And no Company data is exposed
Scenario 14 — Flat JSON / Parameter Wrapping
Given an authenticated User
When Company attributes are submitted according to the supported Rails parameter format
And role is submitted separately
Then the Company attributes are correctly interpreted
And the role is correctly assigned to UserCompany
45. Invariants

The following invariants are mandatory.

INV-01 — Company independence

Company MUST NOT contain user_id.

INV-02 — User independence

User MUST NOT contain company_id.

INV-03 — Explicit relationship

User ↔ Company relationships MUST be represented by UserCompany.

INV-04 — User without Company

A User may exist without any UserCompany.

INV-05 — Multiple Companies

A User may have multiple Companies.

INV-06 — Multiple roles

A User may have multiple roles with the same Company.

INV-07 — Role uniqueness

A (user_id, company_id, role) combination MUST be unique.

INV-08 — Supported roles

Only declared roles may be persisted.

INV-09 — Company name

Company name MUST be present.

INV-10 — SIREN

The SIREN MUST be present, valid, and unique.

INV-11 — SIRET

A SIRET, when present, MUST be valid and unique.

INV-12 — Nullable SIRET

A Company may have a NULL SIRET.

INV-13 — VAT context

vat_regime is contextual data and not a fiscal calculation engine.

INV-14 — Legal form context

legal_form is stored as a string and does not itself implement legal/fiscal rules.

INV-15 — Atomicity

Company + initial UserCompany creation is atomic.

INV-16 — No orphan onboarding Company

The onboarding transaction MUST NOT leave a Company without its required initial relationship.

INV-17 — Soft delete

Company deletion is implemented using deleted_at.

INV-18 — UserCompany soft delete

UserCompany deletion is implemented using deleted_at.

INV-19 — Explicit active scopes

Active records MUST be selected explicitly.

INV-20 — No default scope

No default_scope may be introduced.

INV-21 — Authorization

A User may access only Companies and UserCompany relationships for which the User is authorized.

INV-22 — Parameter separation

Company parameters and UserCompany role parameters MUST remain distinct.

46. Database Constraints

The database MUST enforce the following.

Companies
PRIMARY KEY(id)

UNIQUE(siren)

UNIQUE(siret)

The unique SIRET index must preserve PostgreSQL behavior allowing multiple NULL values.

UserCompanies
FOREIGN KEY(user_id) → users.id

FOREIGN KEY(company_id) → companies.id

UNIQUE(user_id, company_id, role)

The PostgreSQL enum:

user_company_role_enum

is retained.

47. Migration Plan

FC-08 modifies the existing schema.

The migration sequence must be deterministic.

47.1 Companies — SIREN

Current:

siren nullable

Target:

siren NOT NULL

Migration:

change_column_null :companies, :siren, false

